WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.285
Welcome to week three.

2
00:00:01.335 --> 00:00:03.956
The theme is that context is the interface.

3
00:00:04.006 --> 00:00:10.967
Everything the model knows about your task on a given turn is whatever is in its context window at that moment.

4
00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:18.312
This video is about what goes in there, what it costs, and why a well-designed harness treats the window as a budget.

5
00:00:19.062 --> 00:00:21.426
Here is a typical turn, by tokens.

6
00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:24.185
The system prompt and instructions are usually small.

7
00:00:24.235 --> 00:00:29.357
Tool schemas take more room than people expect, and they are sent on every call.

8
00:00:29.407 --> 00:00:33.150
Retrieved documents, if you use retrieval, are often the biggest block.

9
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.253
Then the conversation so far, which grows every turn.

10
00:00:36.303 --> 00:00:42.116
Then the latest tool result, which can be anything from one line to a whole web page.

11
00:00:42.166 --> 00:00:44.874
All of it competes for the same window.

12
00:00:44.924 --> 00:00:48.322
And the window is not just a limit on length.

13
00:00:48.372 --> 00:00:55.564
Models get measurably worse at using what is in the window as it fills up, a pattern researchers call context rot.

14
00:00:55.614 --> 00:00:56.943
More is not better.

15
00:00:56.993 --> 00:00:57.978
Relevant is better.

16
00:00:58.728 --> 00:01:02.596
Anthropic's engineering piece from September 2025 puts it this way.

17
00:01:02.646 --> 00:01:06.514
Prompt engineering was about choosing the words of one instruction.

18
00:01:06.564 --> 00:01:17.484
Context engineering is about choosing everything the model sees on a turn: instructions, tools, documents, history, results, and about keeping that set as small as the task allows.

19
00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:21.011
Their phrase is the smallest set of high-signal tokens.

20
00:01:21.061 --> 00:01:23.753
In practice that means decisions like these.

21
00:01:23.803 --> 00:01:28.063
Which tools does this step need, rather than all of them.

22
00:01:28.113 --> 00:01:31.981
Which ten lines of the document, rather than the document.

23
00:01:32.031 --> 00:01:36.683
A summary of the last forty turns, rather than the forty turns.

24
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:42.205
There are four moves, and you will use all of them in lab three.

25
00:01:42.255 --> 00:01:44.616
The first two reduce what goes in.

26
00:01:44.666 --> 00:01:48.404
Select tools per step rather than giving every step every tool.

27
00:01:48.454 --> 00:01:55.292
Trim tool results to what the next decision needs; a search tool should return titles and snippets, not entire pages.

28
00:01:55.342 --> 00:01:56.670
Retrieve passages, not files.

29
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.803
The other two replace what is in the window with something smaller.

30
00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:03.558
Compaction summarises old turns and drops the originals.

31
00:02:03.608 --> 00:02:09.069
Notes in a file let the agent write down what matters and read it back later.

32
00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:15.613
And a sub-task can run in a fresh context, with only its result coming back to the main loop.

33
00:02:15.663 --> 00:02:19.057
Anthropic's long-running harness from week one used all of these.

34
00:02:19.807 --> 00:02:24.383
When you read a trace this week, look at the context, not just the actions.

35
00:02:24.433 --> 00:02:28.084
How many tokens went in on each turn, and which block grew?

36
00:02:28.134 --> 00:02:34.870
Was the instruction the model ignored sitting near the top of the window, or buried in the middle of a long history?

37
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.496
Did a tool result bring in text that the model then treated as an instruction?

38
00:02:39.546 --> 00:02:42.272
That last one is the seed of week five.

39
00:02:42.322 --> 00:02:44.123
And what did the turn cost?

40
00:02:44.173 --> 00:02:49.057
For most agents, input tokens are most of the bill, and the input is the context.

41
00:02:49.807 --> 00:02:52.571
In lab three, the first task is measurement.

42
00:02:52.621 --> 00:02:57.143
Take your agent's longest trace and chart the tokens per turn, by block.

43
00:02:57.193 --> 00:03:02.067
Then cut the context by a third without losing a result that passed before.

44
00:03:02.117 --> 00:03:04.529
Write down what you removed and why.

45
00:03:04.579 --> 00:03:09.805
Most teams find the savings in tool schemas and in tool results they never trimmed.

46
00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:15.432
The next video is about the biggest block in most windows: retrieval, and why it fails.
